nodecode-orcaslicer is a rewrite of mazzanfar's mazzanfar/hermes-orcaslicer:

  https://github.com/mazzanfar/hermes-orcaslicer
  commit 8fc3a5a40ef4e0c639b76cefd90ad9a48a1a51ec

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 mazzanfar (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's stock-CLI contract with OrcaSlicer (the
--datadir, --slice, --outputdir, --export-3mf, --load-settings and
--load-filaments flags, discovered from a configured path, PATH or a desktop
install), its preset catalog (every machine, process and filament preset
under the install's own roots, with `inherits` resolved the way OrcaSlicer
resolves it), its whitelist of changeable process settings and their bounds,
its job folder (a copy of the input, the resolved presets, slice.log, the
artifacts with their sha256, and the G-code comment report), its export that
never overwrites a file, and the Moonraker, OctoPrint and PrusaLink status
reads and upload bodies, with the rule that a printer credential is named by
an ORCA_ or HERMES_ORCA_ environment variable and never written anywhere.
Seven tools instead of twenty: orca_diagnose, orca_presets, orca_slice,
orca_job, orca_export, orca_printer_status and orca_upload, plus a /orca
command.

What was left out, and why: the original's asynchronous slicing thread (a
cell slices in the call that asked for it, so a job's state is never
"orphaned worker" and needs no second process to poll), its 3MF zip
rewriting for per-object process settings and per-instance
move/rotate/scale (Common Lisp has no zip writer here, and the original's
per-object edits were a large surface of their own), its Bambu LAN protocol
(MQTT plus implicit FTPS, which needs a dependency that does not ride with
Nodecode), its Duet and Flashforge adapters, its camera snapshot and its
SVG layer preview, and the native "open in OrcaSlicer" review copy. A Bambu
printer is reached today by exporting the artifact and using OrcaSlicer's own
upload.
